.TH POWERCURVE 1 "February 2026" "powercurve 0.1.0-rc.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
powercurve \- desktop power management daemon with custom fan curves
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B powercurve
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
powercurve is a power management daemon for Linux desktops. It manages power
profiles (quiet, balanced, performance) and drives fan speeds using configurable
temperature-to-duty curves read from
.IR /etc/powercurve/fan.toml .
.PP
The daemon runs as a systemd service and exposes a D-Bus interface for profile
switching and status queries. Fan curves support per-channel temperature
sources, per-profile overrides, hysteresis, minimum duty floors, stall
detection, and thermal fallback.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B daemon
Run the daemon. Registers the D-Bus service and starts the fan control loop.
Requires root.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress log output (set level to off).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable debug logging.
.RE
.TP
.B profile \fR[\fIname\fR]
Query or set the power profile. Without an argument, prints the current
profile. With an argument (quiet, balanced, performance), switches to that
profile.
.TP
.B status
Display current daemon state: active profile, CPU/GPU temperatures, per-channel
fan duties, RPM readings, active curves, and any active overrides or stall
conditions.
.TP
.B fan \fIchannel duty\fR|\fBclear\fR
Temporarily override a fan channel's duty cycle.
.I duty
is a percentage (0-100).
Use
.B clear
to remove the override and return to curve control. Overrides persist until
cleared or until a profile switch.
.TP
.B fan\-test \fIchannel\fR [\fB\-\-step\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-start\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-settle\fR \fIMS\fR]
Find the minimum duty where a fan channel spins. Ramps duty upward from
.B \-\-start
(default 5%) in
.B \-\-step
(default 5%) increments, waiting
.B \-\-settle
(default 2000ms) at each level and reading RPM. Reports the lowest duty with
non-zero RPM. Requires the daemon to be running.
.TP
.B fan\-detect \fR[\fB\-\-generate\fR]
Detect hwmon devices and print a hardware summary. With
.BR \-\-generate ,
outputs a starter fan.toml config instead.
.TP
.B config
Validate
.I /etc/powercurve/fan.toml
without starting the daemon. Reports errors and warnings. Does not require root.
.TP
.B monitor
Listen for profile switches, thermal events, and fan stalls on D-Bus and send
desktop notifications. Also prints events to stdout. Can run as a standalone
process or as a systemd user service.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The fan config lives at
.IR /etc/powercurve/fan.toml .
Run
.B powercurve fan\-detect \-\-generate
to create a starter config for your hardware.
.PP
The config has four layers of curve resolution (highest priority first):
.IP 1. 4
Per-channel per-profile curves
.IP 2.
Per-channel curves
.IP 3.
Global profile curves
.IP 4.
Shared curve (fallback for all channels)
.PP
See the example configs in
.I /usr/share/doc/powercurve/examples/
or the project README for full documentation.
.SS Channel options
.TP
.B pwm
PWM channel name (e.g. pwm1). Must match the sysfs name under the platform
hwmon.
.TP
.B source
Temperature source: cpu, gpu, or all (max of both).
.TP
.B min_duty
Minimum duty floor as a percentage (0-100). The channel never drops below this
value, preventing fan stalls on hardware with high spin-up thresholds.
.TP
.B stall_detect
Enable RPM-based stall detection (default: false). When duty > 0 but RPM reads
0 for consecutive cycles, bumps duty to min_duty or 15%.
.TP
.B stall_threshold
Consecutive zero-RPM reads before triggering stall recovery (default: 3).
.TP
.B passthrough
Skip this channel entirely (default: false). No duty writes, no RPM reads. The
channel stays under BIOS or firmware control.
.SS Global options
.TP
.B critical_cpu_temp, critical_gpu_temp
Temperature thresholds (Celsius) where all fans go to maximum duty.
.TP
.B hysteresis
Deadband in Celsius for duty reduction (default: 3.0). Prevents rapid duty
changes when temperature hovers near a curve point.
.TP
.B thermal_fallback
Auto-downshift profiles when critical temperatures are hit (default: false).
.TP
.B thermal_cooldown
Seconds to wait at stable temps before stepping back up after thermal fallback
(default: 30).
.TP
.B platform
Hwmon device name to use for PWM outputs. If omitted, auto-detected from
devices with pwm outputs.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/powercurve/fan.toml
Fan configuration file.
.TP
.I /var/lib/powercurve/profile
Persisted power profile state.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the fan config from disk. The daemon validates the new config before
applying it. On failure, the running config is preserved.
.TP
.B SIGTERM
Clean shutdown. All fans are reset to automatic (BIOS) control.
.SH D-BUS INTERFACE
The daemon registers
.B com.vintagetechie.PowerCurve
on the system bus at
.IR /com/vintagetechie/PowerCurve .
See the introspection XML at
.IR /usr/share/dbus-1/interfaces/com.vintagetechie.PowerCurve.xml .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error (connection failure, invalid config, unknown channel, etc.)
.SH EXAMPLES
Check current status:
.PP
.RS
.B powercurve status
.RE
.PP
Switch to quiet profile:
.PP
.RS
.B powercurve profile quiet
.RE
.PP
Override a fan for testing:
.PP
.RS
.B powercurve fan pwm3 50
.RE
.PP
Find a fan's minimum spin-up duty:
.PP
.RS
.B powercurve fan-test pwm1
.RE
.PP
Generate a starter config:
.PP
.RS
.B powercurve fan-detect --generate > fan.toml
.RE
